on":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\n- Reproductible in: Godot v4.4.rc3\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.4.rc3 - Windows 11 (build 22631) - Multi-window, 1 monitor - OpenGL 3 (Compatibility) - AMD Radeon RX 6700 XT (Advanced Micro Devices, Inc.; 32.0.12033.1030) - AMD Ryzen 5 3600 6-Core Processor (12 threads)\n\n### Issue description\n\nI tried to override and move the in-game camera (using middle mouse button) inside the embedded game window. When having stretch mode enabled and resized the window, instead of correctly moving the camera it moves either too fast or too slow and the mouse curser \"wraps\" at unpredictable positions.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Inside a new project, add a sprite to a 2D scene\n2. Set display/window/stretch/mode to either \"canvas_items\" or \"viewport\"\n3. Make sure to have \"Embed Game on Next Play\" and \"Make Game Workspace Floating on Next Play\" enabled\n4. Run the project and resize the window (bug is most noticeable when resizing the window to about half the viewports size)\n5. In the game workspace toolbar, disable game input and enable overriding the in-game camera\n6. Try moving the in-game camera using the middle mouse button\n\nInside the MRP, I already set the window width and height override to half the viewport's size, so you can start the project and proceed at step 5.\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\n[embedded_window_camera_bug.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/19040413/embedded_window_camera_bug.zip)","closed_by":{"login":"Repiteo","id":17489292,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NDg5Mjky","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17489292?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo","html_url":"https://github.com/Repiteo","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo/followers","foll